-- title:
--   Unramified at a height-one prime with ramification index one
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero, $A$ a discrete valuation domain with $L$ as its fraction field, and let $F$, $F'$ be fields that are $L$-algebras and $A$-algebras compatibly, together with an $L$-algebra map $\varphi : F' \to F$ whose underlying ring homomorphism is integral. Let $B$ be an integrally closed domain with fraction field $F$, and $B'$ a Noetherian integrally closed domain with fraction field $F'$, both $A$-algebras compatibly with the inclusions into $F$ and $F'$, and let $B'\to B$ be a module-finite $A$-algebra map compatible with $\varphi$, in the sense that for every $x \in B'$ the image of $x$ in $F$ via $B$ equals $\varphi$ of the image of $x$ in $F'$. Let $Q \subset B$ be a prime ideal of height $1$. Assume that every place $w$ of $F$ over $L$ (a proper valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $L$ and a principal ideal ring) which is centred at $Q$, i.e. $\operatorname{ord}_w(r)\ge 0$ for all $r \in B$ and, for $r \in B$ nonzero, $\operatorname{ord}_w(r) > 0$ exactly when $r \in Q$, has ramification index $1$ along $\varphi$: the least positive integer $n$ for which some nonzero $f \in F'$ satisfies $\operatorname{ord}_w(\varphi f) = n$ equals $1$. Assume finally that the maximal ideal of $A$ is generated by an element $\varpi$ whose image in $B$ does not lie in $Q$. Then $B$ is unramified over $B'$ at $Q$, i.e. the localisation of $B$ at $Q$ is formally unramified over $B'$.
--
--   This is the form of the criterion "a module-finite extension of normal domains is unramified at a horizontal height-one prime whose associated place has ramification index one" in which the ramification hypothesis is imposed only at the places centred at the given prime $Q$ rather than over a whole chart. It is used in the study of full-level modular curves, where unramifiedness is verified at height-one primes not containing the image of the uniformiser of the base discrete valuation ring.
